// TEST: T^0 call whose later argument would NARROW (rejects)
// ERROR: BL3999
// ERROR-CONTAINS: would have to NARROW into it
// ============================================================================
// Compatibility at this seam is judged by what the emitted MONOMORPH accepts,
// not by type equality: the specialization is built from the FIRST teaching,
// so a later argument is fine exactly when it flows into that signature
// without conversion.
//
// Here argument 1 makes `T` Int64, so the monomorph is
// `int64 add0(int64, int64)` -- and feeding it 2.5 is the narrowing g++
// refuses under -Werror=float-conversion. The reverse order (`add0(2.5, 3)`)
// builds `double add0(double, double)` and WIDENS the int64, which C++ does
// silently and which is therefore accepted; 044 pins that direction.
// ============================================================================
function add0(a: T^0, b: T^0) -> T^0 = a + b

let r = add0(3, 2.5)
